## Objects

The record is an object: `$` is the whole record, and each field is one of its keys. An object is written `{"a": 1, "b": 2}`. `o[k]` reads the key that `k` holds on each row.

`{ip: str, port: i64}` is closed: converting a value to it with `as` keeps the fields it names and drops the rest. `{ip: str, ...}` is open and keeps them.

Code

```
closed = obj as {ip: str};
open = obj as {ip: str, ...};
text = obj as {ip: str, port: str};
```

Input

```
{
  "obj": {
    "ip": "a",
    "port": 1
  }
}
```

→

Output

```
{
  "obj": {
    "ip": "a",
    "port": 1
  },
  "closed": {
    "ip": "a"
  },
  "open": {
    "ip": "a",
    "port": 1
  },
  "text": {
    "ip": "a",
    "port": "1"
  }
}
```

Writing a field by name adds it to either kind. A computed key, such as `o[key]`, names a field only the record knows, so it writes only to an open object: one declared with `...`, or one you build without declaring its type. A computed key written to a closed object is an error when you save.

-   `o: {ip: str} = {ip: "a"}; o[key] = 1;` does not compile. Write `o: {...} = o as {...}`.

## Before you start

-   Have a bucket in S3, or in a store that speaks its API, including conditional writes: a write with `If-None-Match: *` must succeed only when no object is at its key.
-   The table is the one at `location`, and the destination creates a missing one there. Or it is the external table `unityCatalog` names, which must exist: see [Unity Catalog](#unity-catalog). A table that Unity Catalog manages is not written.
-   A table that exists is one this writer can append to:
    -   its protocol needs reader version 3 or lower and writer version 7 or lower, and each writer feature it names is one of `appendOnly`, `invariants`, `checkConstraints`, `changeDataFeed`, `columnMapping`, `v2Checkpoint`, `domainMetadata`, `deletionVectors`, `vacuumProtocolCheck` and `timestampNtz`;
    -   its files are Parquet;
    -   it maps columns by name, with `delta.columnMapping.maxColumnId` set, or not at all;
    -   it sets no check constraint;
    -   no column is generated, an identity, has a default or carries an invariant, and every column is nullable, as is every array's element;
    -   it has a column a data file can hold, one that is not `void` alone;
    -   its checkpoints keep file statistics as JSON: `delta.checkpoint.writeStatsAsJson` is not `false` and `delta.checkpoint.writeStatsAsStruct` is not `true`;
    -   it is partitioned by columns of type `boolean`, `byte`, `short`, `integer`, `long`, `double`, `date`, `timestamp` or `string`.The destination refuses to start on any other, and says why.
-   Give whatever signs for storage the permissions [Storage permissions](#permissions) lists: the access key or role the destination names, else the worker host's AWS credentials. Through Unity Catalog, the credentials it vends must carry them.
-   Send this destination records of one type: the routes writing to it write the same record, which is the table's schema. See [Columns](#columns).

## Storage permissions

Whatever signs for storage needs, under the table's location:

-   `s3:ListBucket` on the table's `_delta_log/`;
-   `s3:GetObject`, `s3:PutObject` and `s3:DeleteObject`;
-   `s3:AbortMultipartUpload`.

A policy for the table in the example:

```
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": [
        "s3:ListBucket"
      ],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::lake",
      "Condition": {
        "StringLike": {
          "s3:prefix": "tables/access_log/_delta_log/*"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": [
        "s3:GetObject",
        "s3:PutObject",
        "s3:DeleteObject",
        "s3:AbortMultipartUpload"
      ],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::lake/tables/access_log/*"
    }
  ]
}
```

With `roleArn`, the role carries this policy and the credentials the destination starts from may assume it.

### Set up Databricks

Create the service principal and its OAuth secret as Databricks' [OAuth machine-to-machine authentication](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/auth/oauth-m2m) describes, and give it access to the table as [credential vending for external system access](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/external-access/credential-vending) describes. Then:

-   `workspace` is the workspace's URL;
-   `table` is the table's name, `catalog.schema.table`;
-   `clientId` is the service principal's client id;
-   `clientSecret` is the [credential](/docs/secrets) holding its OAuth secret;
-   `region`, beside `unityCatalog`, is the region of the table's bucket.

### What is refused

The configuration is refused when a Delta destination:

-   names both `location` and `unityCatalog`, or neither;
-   gives a `workspace` that is not an `https://` address with nothing after its host, or an `http://` one off this host;
-   names a `table` that is not three parts, `catalog.schema.table`;
-   names no `clientId`;
-   names no `region` beside `unityCatalog`;
-   names `accessKeyId`, `secretAccessKey`, `roleArn` or `authEndpoint` beside `unityCatalog`: the destination takes its storage credentials from Unity Catalog.

The destination refuses to start on a table that is not Delta, not external, or not in S3, and on one reached through a multi-region access point.

## Example

Posts each batch as a JSON array, and reads the `DD-API-KEY` header's value from the worker-host variable `INTAKE_API_KEY`.

```
{
  "id": "intake",
  "type": "http",
  "url": "https://intake.example.com/v1/logs",
  "body": "jsonArray",
  "headers": {
    "X-Source": "smeltr"
  },
  "credentials": {
    "DD-API-KEY": "INTAKE_API_KEY"
  }
}
```

![The HTTP form on the Destinations page: id intake, its URL, batched requests as a JSON array, and the header X-Source.](/http-destination-form-light.png)

The Destinations page's HTTP form. Choose batched or one record per request, then the format. Credentials name a secret; Advanced holds the rest.

## Example

Sends to a Mimir tenant, and reads the `Authorization` header's value from the credential `PROMETHEUS_TOKEN`.

```
{
  "id": "metrics-out",
  "type": "prometheus",
  "url": "https://mimir.example.com/api/v1/push",
  "headers": {
    "X-Scope-OrgID": "tenant-1"
  },
  "credentials": {
    "Authorization": "PROMETHEUS_TOKEN"
  }
}
```

## Records it gives

Each `index` or `create` action's document becomes one record: the document as it was sent. This request gives two records:

```
{"index":{"_index":"web","_id":"a1"}}
{"message":"GET /cart 200","host":"web-1"}
{"create":{"_index":"web"}}
{"message":"GET /cart 500","host":"web-2"}
```

The receiver takes no `update` or `delete`. Each is answered `400` in its own item, and the request's other actions are taken all the same. So is a document that is not a JSON object.

## Indexer acknowledgement

Each token sets its own acknowledgement. Set `acknowledgement: true` on the token of a sender that has indexer acknowledgement turned on:

```
{
  "vector": {
    "token": "VECTOR_HEC_TOKEN",
    "acknowledgement": true
  },
  "fluentbit": {
    "token": "FLUENTBIT_HEC_TOKEN"
  }
}
```

That sender names a channel, in the `X-Splunk-Request-Channel` header or the `channel` query. The receiver answers at once with an ID, `{"text":"Success","code":0,"ackId":7}`, and the sender asks after it at `/services/collector/ack`. The ID reads `true` once every destination has the request's events, and the receiver forgets it once the sender has heard that.

An ID reads `false` while its events are on their way, and also when the worker asked does not hold it, because another worker gave it or the worker restarted. The sender then sends the request again: a duplicate, never a loss. A balancer in front of the workers must therefore keep each sender's connections on one worker, so a channel's requests and its ack queries reach the same worker.

The receiver holds the request of a sender whose token lacks `acknowledgement` until its events are delivered, whether or not it names a channel. A sender that names a channel but never asks after an `ackId` needs such a token.

## Example

Reads the table `events` in the database `auth` on `db.internal`, as the user `smeltr_reader`, whose password is in the worker-host variable `MYSQL_PASSWORD`. It begins at the largest `event_id` on its first poll, and each poll reads the rows whose `event_id` is past the last one sent.

```
{
  "id": "auth-events",
  "type": "mysql",
  "host": "db.internal",
  "database": "auth",
  "user": "smeltr_reader",
  "password": "MYSQL_PASSWORD",
  "query": "SELECT event_id, occurred_at, user_name FROM events",
  "mode": {
    "type": "incrementing",
    "position": [
      "event_id"
    ]
  },
  "fields": [
    {
      "name": "event_id",
      "type": "i64"
    },
    {
      "name": "occurred_at",
      "type": "datetime"
    },
    {
      "name": "user_name",
      "type": "str"
    }
  ]
}
```

![The MySQL / MariaDB form on the Sources page: id auth-events, host db.internal, database auth, user smeltr_reader, and the query SELECT event_id, occurred_at, user_name FROM events.](/mysql-source-form-light.png)

The same source in the Sources page's add dialog, before you pick its password or run its query.

