## Senders it takes

Any sender of remote\_write 1.0 or 2.0 works.

-   Prometheus's [remote\_write](https://prometheus.io/docs/prometheus/latest/configuration/configuration/#remote_write), in server or agent mode. Set `protobuf_message: io.prometheus.write.v2.Request` to send 2.0, which carries each series' metadata and start time.
-   Grafana Alloy's `prometheus.remote_write`.
-   VictoriaMetrics' `vmagent`.
-   The OpenTelemetry Collector's `prometheusremotewrite` exporter, exponential histograms included.

Grafana Alloy sends to it what its scrape components forward to `prometheus.remote_write.smeltr.receiver`, with `PROM_TOKEN` set in its environment:

```
prometheus.remote_write "smeltr" {
  endpoint {
    url = "https://workers.example.com:9090/api/v1/write"
    bearer_token = sys.env("PROM_TOKEN")
    tls_config {
      ca_file = "/etc/alloy/smeltr-ca.pem"
    }
  }
}
```

## Send from syslog-ng

This example uses syslog-ng's [syslog()](https://syslog-ng.github.io/admin-guide/070_Destinations/170_syslog/README) driver over TLS and checks the receiver's certificate against `smeltr-ca.pem`:

```
destination d_smeltr {
  syslog("worker1.example.com"
    transport("tls")
    port(6514)
    tls(ca-file("/etc/syslog-ng/smeltr-ca.pem"))
  );
};

log { source(s_src); destination(d_smeltr); };
```

For a client certificate, add `cert-file()` and `key-file()` to `tls()`. Without TLS, use `transport("tcp")` and the plain port.

To a UDP receiver, use `transport("udp")`:

```
destination d_smeltr {
  network("worker1.example.com"
    transport("udp")
    port(514)
  );
};
```

## Parse the header

The receiver does not parse the header, since every vendor writes syslog its own way. A [Parser step](/docs/steps/parser) with the [Regex/Grok](/docs/steps/parser#grok-matching) format reads it in the pipeline, where each source's quirks can have a pattern of their own. This step reads an RFC 5424 header with its first pattern and an RFC 3164 header with its second:

```
{
  "id": "syslog-header",
  "type": "parser",
  "input": "_raw",
  "kind": {
    "type": "grok",
    "patterns": [
      "^<%{INT:pri}>%{INT:version} %{TIMESTAMP_ISO8601:time} %{NOTSPACE:host} %{NOTSPACE:app} %{NOTSPACE:procid} %{NOTSPACE:msgid} (?<sd>-|(?:\\[[^\\]]*\\])+)(?: %{GREEDYDATA:message})?$",
      "^<%{INT:pri}>%{SYSLOGTIMESTAMP:time} %{SYSLOGHOST:host} %{DATA:app}(?:\\[%{POSINT:procid}\\])?: %{GREEDYDATA:message}$"
    ]
  },
  "declared": [
    {
      "name": "pri",
      "type": "i64"
    },
    {
      "name": "time",
      "type": "str"
    },
    {
      "name": "host",
      "type": "str"
    },
    {
      "name": "app",
      "type": "str"
    },
    {
      "name": "procid",
      "type": "str"
    },
    {
      "name": "msgid",
      "type": "str"
    },
    {
      "name": "sd",
      "type": "str"
    },
    {
      "name": "message",
      "type": "str"
    }
  ]
}
```

Given these two messages as `_raw`:

```
<165>1 2026-10-11T22:14:15.003Z fw01.example.com sshd 4123 AUTH [origin ip="10.20.0.7"] Accepted publickey for deploy
```

```
<34>Oct 11 22:14:15 fw01 su: 'su root' failed for deploy on /dev/pts/8
```

it gives these fields:

```
{
  "pri": 165,
  "version": "1",
  "time": "2026-10-11T22:14:15.003Z",
  "host": "fw01.example.com",
  "app": "sshd",
  "procid": "4123",
  "msgid": "AUTH",
  "sd": "[origin ip=\"10.20.0.7\"]",
  "message": "Accepted publickey for deploy"
}
```

```
{
  "pri": 34,
  "time": "Oct 11 22:14:15",
  "host": "fw01",
  "app": "su",
  "message": "'su root' failed for deploy on /dev/pts/8"
}
```

The facility is `pri / 8` and the severity `pri % 8`: 165 is facility 20, `local4`, at severity 5, notice. To make `time` a datetime, declare it with a format; see [Parser step](/docs/steps/parser).

## Example

Pipeline `stamp-ecs`

```
{
  "id": "stamp",
  "type": "eval",
  "assignments": [{ "target": "ecs.version", "expr": "\"8.11\"" }]
}
```

Step

  Saved JSON

```
{ "id": "call", "type": "call", "pipeline": "stamp-ecs" }
```

Input

```
{
  "status": 503,
  "path": "/pay"
}
```

```
{
  "status": 200,
  "path": "/home"
}
```

→

Output

```
{
  "status": 503,
  "path": "/pay",
  "ecs": {
    "version": "8.11"
  }
}
```

```
{
  "status": 200,
  "path": "/home",
  "ecs": {
    "version": "8.11"
  }
}
```

This pipeline calls `stamp-ecs`, which holds one Eval step that stamps every record with the ECS version.

### Choose a value

[`if`](/docs/code-reference#statements) and `else` choose which statements run for each record. For a short value selection, write `severity = status >= 500 ? "error" : "normal";`.

Code

```
if (status >= 500) {
  severity = "error";
} else {
  severity = "normal";
}
```

Input

```
{
  "status": 503
}
```

→

Output

```
{
  "status": 503,
  "severity": "error"
}
```

[`switch`](/docs/code-reference#statements) compares one value with each `case` in order. Cases with no statements between them share the next one's, and `break` ends the switch:

Code

```
switch (status) {
  case 200:
  case 204:
    outcome = "ok";
    break;
  case 404:
    outcome = "missing";
    break;
  default:
    outcome = "error";
}
```

Input

```
{ "status": 204 }
```

```
{ "status": 404 }
```

```
{ "status": 503 }
```

→

Output

```
{ "outcome": "ok", "status": 204 }
```

```
{ "outcome": "missing", "status": 404 }
```

```
{ "outcome": "error", "status": 503 }
```

### Work with arrays

[`filter`](/docs/code-reference#filter) keeps the items of an array for which an arrow's expression is true. [`map`](/docs/code-reference#map) replaces each item with the arrow's value. The original array remains unless you assign the result back to it, and the calls chain: `values.filter(x => x > 2).map(x => x * 2)`.

Code

```
values = [1, 2, 3, 4];
kept = values.filter(x => x > 2);
doubled = kept.map(x => x * 2);
```

Input

```
{
  "id": 7
}
```

→

Output

```
{
  "id": 7,
  "values": [1,2,3,4],
  "kept": [3,4],
  "doubled": [6,8]
}
```

[`for...of`](/docs/code-reference#statements) visits each item in an array.

Code

```
let total = 0;
for (let value of [4, 5, 6]) {
  total = total + value;
}
sum = total;
```

Input

```
{
  "id": 7
}
```

→

Output

```
{
  "id": 7,
  "sum": 15
}
```

### Reuse a calculation

A [local function](/docs/code-reference#statements) names a repeated calculation. The body's last expression, written without a semicolon, is its value, as is a `return`.

Code

```
function addTax(amount: f64): f64 {
  return amount * 1.2;
}
price = addTax(10);
```

Input

```
{
  "id": 7
}
```

→

Output

```
{
  "id": 7,
  "price": 12.0
}
```

### Handle bad data

Use [`items ?? []`](/docs/code-reference#nulls) when a missing array should default to an empty one. Only the selected branch can mark the record with an exception. For example, `d == 0 ? 0 : n / d` returns zero when `d` is zero, without a `DivideByZero`. A [`try`](/docs/code-reference#exceptions) answers what is left, one kind at a time:

Code

```
try {
  ratio = bytes / packets;
  count = parseInt(text);
} catch (e: DivideByZero | NaN) {
  ratio = 0.0;
} catch (e) {
  problem = e.kind;
}
```

Input

```
{
  "bytes": 1200,
  "packets": 0,
  "text": "many"
}
```

→

Output

```
{
  "bytes": 1200,
  "packets": 0,
  "ratio": 0.0,
  "text": "many"
}
```

Dividing by zero stops the `try` before `parseInt` runs, so the first catch takes the record. A record with packets but unreadable text would run the second catch.

## Example

Step

  Saved JSON

```
{
  "id": "branch",
  "type": "if",
  "condition": "status >= 500",
  "then": [
    {
      "id": "severity-error",
      "type": "eval",
      "assignments": [{ "target": "severity", "expr": "\"error\"" }]
    },
    {
      "id": "page",
      "type": "eval",
      "assignments": [{ "target": "page", "expr": "true" }]
    }
  ],
  "else": [
    {
      "id": "severity-normal",
      "type": "eval",
      "assignments": [{ "target": "severity", "expr": "\"normal\"" }]
    },
    {
      "id": "drop-health",
      "type": "drop",
      "condition": "path == \"/health\""
    }
  ]
}
```

Input

```
{
  "status": 503,
  "path": "/pay"
}
```

```
{
  "status": 200,
  "path": "/home"
}
```

```
{
  "status": 200,
  "path": "/health"
}
```

→

Output

```
{
  "status": 503,
  "path": "/pay",
  "severity": "error",
  "page": true
}
```

```
{
  "status": 200,
  "path": "/home",
  "severity": "normal"
}
```

The parser declares `status` as `i64` and `path` as `str`. The 503 record takes the then branch; the two 200 records take the else branch, where the Drop step removes the `/health` record.

### Null and missing fields

A condition that is null is not true, so the record takes the else branch. A record without `status` does:

Step

  Saved JSON

```
{
  "id": "branch",
  "type": "if",
  "condition": "status >= 500",
  "then": [
    {
      "id": "severity-error",
      "type": "eval",
      "assignments": [{ "target": "severity", "expr": "\"error\"" }]
    }
  ],
  "else": [
    {
      "id": "severity-normal",
      "type": "eval",
      "assignments": [{ "target": "severity", "expr": "\"normal\"" }]
    }
  ]
}
```

Input

```
{
  "path": "/pay"
}
```

→

Output

```
{
  "path": "/pay",
  "severity": "normal"
}
```

## Example

Step

  Saved JSON

```
{
  "id": "rename",
  "type": "rename",
  "pairs": [
    { "from": "src", "to": "source.ip" },
    { "from": "msg", "to": "message" }
  ]
}
```

Input

```
{
  "src": "10.0.0.1",
  "msg": "login ok",
  "status": 200
}
```

→

Output

```
{
  "message": "login ok",
  "source": {
    "ip": "10.0.0.1"
  },
  "status": 200
}
```

